[8]
       65504           8           0            2            8.137e-03   True         0cac45ca

    data_max=1 keeps 4 initializers in FP32, data_max=65504 keeps 0.
    So 'which nodes are sensitive' is a statement about `data_max`, not a property of
    the model alone. This CNN's activations peak around 3.3, so any threshold at or
    above 16 classifies every node the same way.

    data_max [16, 512, 65504] all produce the **byte-identical** graph (0cac45ca),
    and their measured errors are 4.126e-03, 7.488e-03, 8.137e-03.
    They disagree, on identical bytes.
    Any difference between those rows is TensorRT build nondeterminism, not the knob.
    Without the graph hash a lucky middle row reads as a sweet spot -- it is not one.
    **When a sweep changes a number, check whether it changed the artefact first.**

    data_max=1 keeps the *most* in FP32 (4 initializers against 0) and pays
    3 Cast nodes against 2. Its error this run is 7.488e-03 against 8.137e-03.
    Pinning nodes to FP32 inside an FP16 graph is not free accuracy: every extra
    boundary is another round trip through FP16, and across runs this row has landed
    on both sides of the others. The Cast count is the deterministic part of that
    statement, so it is what this case asserts; the error ordering sits inside the
    build noise measured just above and is deliberately *not* asserted.
